{% llm_copy_prompt title="Skip unaffected split e2e tests in this workspace" %}
You are scoping split (atomized) e2e test files in an Nx workspace so that each test file's task reruns only when the code it tests changes. Read this guide first: {pageUrl}

1. Find the e2e projects and their split targets with `nx show project <e2e-project> --json`. Split targets are named `e2e-ci--<test file path>`. If there are none, stop. Cypress creates them only when the `nxE2EPreset` options set `ciWebServerCommand`, and Vitest and Cypress component tests don't read the directives.
2. Before editing any files, ask the user to choose a scoping approach and wait for their answer:
   - **A. Project-based scoping with `@nx-depends-on` (recommended).** Explicitly list the projects each test exercises. The projects they depend on are included automatically.
   - **B. Import-based scoping with `@nx-ignore-server-inputs` (advanced).** Use only when we understand exactly what each test depends on, Ultracache is enabled in Nx Cloud, the runner is Playwright or Cypress, and the imports capture everything needed to invalidate its cached result.
   - Explain that `@nx-ignore-server-inputs` can replay stale results when dependencies are missed. Do not select it automatically.
3. Inspect each test and identify its dependencies before recommending `@nx-ignore-server-inputs`. Call out anything imports cannot reliably capture, including CSS, assets, API servers, app shell code, configuration, environment variables, and runtime inputs. If coverage is uncertain, recommend `@nx-depends-on` or leave the test unscoped.
   - For project-based scoping, use `// @nx-depends-on: <project>, <project>`. List every project the test exercises. Don't list the app project just to cover missing dependencies, because that puts the whole app back in the hash; leave whole-app tests unscoped instead.
   - Use `@nx-depends-on` when the test depends on stylesheets (screenshots, visibility checks) outside Cypress with the Vite preprocessor, on files loaded by path (`templateUrl`, `styleUrl`, static assets, fetched data), on the app shell, layouts or shared providers, on an API server, or on the server task's environment variables, runtime inputs or external dependencies.
   - Leave smoke tests that exercise the whole app unscoped.
4. Put the directive at the top of the file, before the first line of code, at the start of a comment line. `@nx-depends-on` needs the colon right after its name.
5. With `@nx-ignore-server-inputs`, add bare library subpath imports of the actual feature modules, such as `import '@myorg/checkout/checkout-page';`:
   - Avoid relative cross-project imports, route tables, application entry points, and barrels. Route tables, application entry points, and barrels pull unrelated features into the hash.
   - If a needed subpath is not exported, explain the required package-export change instead of using a relative cross-project import.
   - For lazy-loaded features, import the feature module directly. Playwright doesn't read modules that a dynamic `import()` loads.
   - Playwright loads imports in Node. A module that uses `window` or `document` at the top level, a stylesheet in an ECMAScript module package, or a `.vue` file fails the whole test file. Cypress with webpack fails to bundle stylesheets and `.vue` files. If a feature won't load, import the TypeScript modules it uses, or switch the file to `@nx-depends-on`.
   - If importing the available modules cannot reliably cover the test's dependencies, recommend `@nx-depends-on` or leave the test unscoped.

6. Fix the tooling errors the imports cause:
   - `@nx/enforce-module-boundaries` skips most checks for bare imports in files with `@nx-ignore-server-inputs`, but still checks imports that name values or types.
   - Disable `import/no-unassigned-import` (eslint-plugin-import or the Oxlint import plugin) for the e2e test files instead of removing the imports.
   - If a test file imports `.tsx` files, set `jsx` (for example `"react-jsx"`) in the e2e project's TypeScript configuration. A missing `jsx` shows up as TS6305 with project references or TS6142 with path aliases.
7. Verify with `nx show target inputs <e2e-project>:<split target> --check <file the test exercises> <file it doesn't>`. With `@nx-depends-on`, the first file is an input and the second isn't. For `@nx-ignore-server-inputs`, run the target in CI, then inspect its Ultracache configuration in the Nx Cloud UI. Check that it includes the files the test exercises and excludes unrelated files. If the configuration isn't scoped as expected:
   - Playwright: Nx runs the `webServer` as a task only when `reuseExistingServer` is `true`, the entry sets no `wait` or `env`, and `command` is `nx run <project>:<target>` or `nx <target> <project>` with no other arguments. Nx evaluates the config with its own environment, so `reuseExistingServer: !process.env.CI` is `false` in CI. Without a server task, `@nx-ignore-server-inputs` has no effect.
   - An `nx.json` target default that matches the split target, such as one keyed `e2e-ci--*`, replaces the inferred `inputs` and `dependsOn`. Add `"..."` to each array it sets, and set `"inputs": false` on any server dependency it adds.
   - If the test needs server environment variables, runtime inputs or external dependencies that the imports do not capture, report the limitation and recommend `@nx-depends-on` only if it covers those dependencies; otherwise leave the test unscoped.
8. Do not create shared dependency groups, named inputs, target defaults, or blanket dependency lists to compensate for missing coverage. For example, do not introduce a `shopE2eRuntime` input. Report the limitation instead of inventing shared configuration.
9. Prefer directives over `project.json` entries. A `project.json` entry under the split target's name replaces the inferred `inputs` and `dependsOn`, so copy every entry you keep and don't add `"..."`. The entry stops matching when the test file moves.

Notes:

- Without a recorded Ultracache run (local machines, the first CI run, or `NX_CLOUD_USE_ULTRACACHE=false`), a task with `@nx-ignore-server-inputs` hashes its declared inputs minus the server task's inputs.
- Without Ultracache, every split task hashes the whole e2e project, so an edit to any test file reruns all of them.
- Ultracache records reads as folder patterns per kind of file (JavaScript and TypeScript together, other files by extension). A new file under a folder the test read from changes the hash. Existing files the test didn't read don't.
- `nx affected -t e2e-ci` still selects `e2e-ci` by its unscoped inputs. Each split task whose hash didn't change replays from the cache.
- A test file with both directives is scoped by projects.

Report which test files you scoped, the directive and imports you added to each, and any test file you left unscoped and why.
{% /llm_copy_prompt %}

The [Atomizer](/docs/kb/split-e2e-tasks) splits an end-to-end (e2e) suite into one task per test file, such as `e2e-ci--src/checkout.spec.ts`.
Nx caches each of those tasks, but by default each task's hash covers the whole app and the whole e2e project.
A change to any feature, or to any test file, reruns every test file.

For example, a `shop` app has `checkout/`, `search/`, `cart/`, and `shared/` folders, and its e2e project has two test files, `checkout.spec.ts` and `search.spec.ts`.
Before, without a directive, `checkout.spec.ts` and `search.spec.ts` both hash every folder, so an edit to `search/` reruns both:

![Without a directive, checkout.spec.ts and search.spec.ts both hash checkout/, search/, cart/, and shared/, so an edit in search/ reruns both tests.](../../../assets/guides/running-tasks/e2e-hash-before.svg)

To fix that, add a one-line directive comment at the top of a test file.
The directive tells Nx which code the test depends on.
When a change doesn't touch that code, Nx skips the test and replays its cached result.

After, with a directive in each test file, `checkout.spec.ts` hashes only `checkout/` and `shared/`, and `search.spec.ts` hashes only `search/` and `shared/`.
The same edit to `search/` reruns only `search.spec.ts`:

![With a directive, checkout.spec.ts hashes checkout/ and shared/, and search.spec.ts hashes search/ and shared/, so an edit in search/ reruns only search.spec.ts.](../../../assets/guides/running-tasks/e2e-hash-after.svg)

## The two directives

`// @nx-depends-on: <projects>` scopes the test to the projects you list and the projects they depend on.
The test reruns when one of those projects changes.
It works in Playwright, Cypress, and Jest test files.

`// @nx-ignore-server-inputs` drops the app server from the test's hash.
With [Ultracache](/docs/reference/project-configuration#ultracache) turned on in Nx Cloud, Nx then hashes the test from the files its last CI run read: the test file and the app code it imports.
The test reruns when one of those files changes.
It works in Playwright and Cypress test files.

Without Ultracache, each test also hashes every file in the e2e project, so an edit to any test file still reruns it.

Start with `@nx-depends-on` to explicitly scope each test to the projects it exercises and their dependencies.
Use `@nx-ignore-server-inputs` only when Ultracache is on and you have verified that imports capture everything needed to invalidate the test's cached result. Missing dependencies can cause stale results to be replayed.
If you cannot establish complete coverage with either directive, leave the test unscoped.

## Examples

Both examples test the checkout page from the `checkout` library, which the `shop` application uses.

### Using `@nx-depends-on`

```ts
// apps/shop-e2e/src/checkout.spec.ts
// @nx-depends-on: checkout
import { expect, test } from '@playwright/test';

test('shows the order summary', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/checkout');
  await expect(
    page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Order summary' })
  ).toBeVisible();
});
```

The test reruns when `checkout` or a project it depends on changes.
An edit to the unrelated `search` library replays the cached result.

### Using `@nx-ignore-server-inputs`

```ts
// apps/shop-e2e/src/checkout.spec.ts
// @nx-ignore-server-inputs
import '@myorg/checkout/checkout-page';
import { expect, test } from '@playwright/test';

test('shows the order summary', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/checkout');
  await expect(
    page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Order summary' })
  ).toBeVisible();
});
```

The bare import makes the test runner load the checkout library's page when it loads the test file.
Ultracache records those reads, so the test reruns when the checkout page, or a module it imports, changes.

Import the feature's own page or component, not the route table, the app entry point, or a barrel file.
Those pull every feature into the hash.
See [choose what to import](#choose-what-to-import) for more.

### Keep a smoke test unscoped

Leave the directive off a test that exercises the whole app, such as a login-and-checkout smoke test.
It reruns on any change to the app.

## Directive syntax

Put each directive on its own comment line at the top of the test file, before the first line of code.
Line comments and block comments both work, and blank lines, a `#!` line, or other comments can come first:

```ts
// apps/shop-e2e/src/checkout.spec.ts
// @nx-depends-on: checkout, payments
// @nx-depends-on: tag:scope:shared
import { test } from '@playwright/test';
```

```ts
/**
 * Checkout flow tests.
 * @nx-ignore-server-inputs
 */
import '@myorg/checkout/checkout-page';
```

`@nx-depends-on` takes a colon and a comma-separated list of project names or patterns, such as `tag:scope:shared`.
Repeat the line to list more.
`@nx-ignore-server-inputs` takes no value.

A test file can have both directives, and Nx then scopes it by projects, because `@nx-depends-on` also leaves out the server task's inputs.

## Check the result

### Check `@nx-depends-on` on your machine

Run `nx show target inputs` for the test file's split task, and pass `--check` with a file the test exercises and a file it doesn't:

```shell
nx show target inputs shop-e2e:e2e-ci--src/checkout.spec.ts --check libs/checkout/src/index.ts libs/search/src/index.ts
```

For a test file with `// @nx-depends-on: checkout`, Nx lists the `checkout` file as an input and the `search` file as not an input:

```text
✓ These arguments were inputs for shop-e2e:e2e-ci--src/checkout.spec.ts:
  libs/checkout/src/index.ts

✗ These arguments were not inputs for shop-e2e:e2e-ci--src/checkout.spec.ts:
  libs/search/src/index.ts
```

The listed projects stay in the hash with or without Ultracache, so the result matches CI.
On your machine, the inputs also include every file in the e2e project, such as `search.spec.ts`.
In CI with Ultracache, Nx keeps only the e2e project files the test read.

### Check `@nx-ignore-server-inputs` in CI

Run the split e2e target in CI so Ultracache records its file inputs.
Then inspect the target's Ultracache configuration in the Nx Cloud UI to see which files the test depends on.

For `e2e-ci--src/checkout.spec.ts`, check that it includes the test file, the checkout library,
and the modules it imports, without unrelated files from the search library.

## Additional considerations

Read these sections when a directive doesn't skip the tests you expect, or when you're unsure which directive fits.

### Choose between the directives

`@nx-ignore-server-inputs` fits a test when all the code it exercises is in modules the test file can import, such as pages, components, and the services they call.
Use `@nx-depends-on` instead when the test's results depend on something the test runner doesn't read:

- Files the app loads by path instead of importing them, such as static assets, data it fetches at runtime, and Angular `templateUrl` and `styleUrl` files.
- App code the test exercises but doesn't import, such as the app shell, layouts, and shared providers.
- Stylesheets, for screenshot comparisons and visibility checks.
  Only Cypress with the Vite preprocessor reads the stylesheets that imports reach.
- Other services the app calls, such as an API server.
- The server task's configuration files, environment variables, runtime inputs, and external dependencies.

### Choose what to import

The test runner reads each module the test file imports, and each module those modules import.

Decide what each test file is meant to test, then use bare library subpath imports of the actual feature modules that cover that scope, such as `import '@myorg/checkout/checkout-page';`.
This may require multiple imports. Avoid relative cross-project imports, route tables, application entry points, and barrels.
If a needed subpath is not exported by the library, explain the required package-export change before using it.

Lazy-loaded modules sometimes belong in that scope, but often represent other parts of the app that the test doesn't exercise.
Include them only when they are part of what the test is meant to cover.

Playwright doesn't read modules reached through dynamic `import()`, so import a lazy-loaded module directly when the test covers it.
Cypress bundles dynamic imports, so importing a route table can pull unrelated lazy-loaded features into the test's hash.

Playwright loads imported modules in Node.
Cypress bundles them with its preprocessor: Vite when the `nxE2EPreset` options set `bundler: 'vite'`, webpack otherwise.

| File an import reaches                                  | Playwright                                                 | Cypress with webpack | Cypress with Vite          |
| ------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------- |
| A module that a dynamic `import()` loads                | Not read                                                   | Read                 | Read                       |
| A stylesheet                                            | Not read, or fails to load in an ECMAScript module package | Fails to bundle      | Read                       |
| A `.vue` file                                           | Fails to load                                              | Fails to bundle      | Fails without a Vue plugin |
| A file a component names by path, such as `templateUrl` | Not read                                                   | Not read             | Not read                   |

In Playwright, a module that fails to load, or that uses `window` or `document` in its top-level code, fails the test file before its tests run.
With a custom Cypress preprocessor, its configuration decides what it reads.
For a feature that doesn't load, import the TypeScript modules it uses, or scope the test with `@nx-depends-on`.

### Without a recorded run

With Ultracache enabled, Nx 24 and later automatically use recorded file inputs in CI when a recording is available for the task.
Set `NX_CLOUD_USE_ULTRACACHE=false` to use declared inputs instead. See [Ultracache and Nx versions](/docs/kb/ultracache-nx-versions).

Without a recorded run, such as on your machine, a task with `@nx-ignore-server-inputs` hashes its declared inputs without the server task's inputs.
Each split task's declared inputs cover the whole e2e project, so an edit to any test file reruns every test file.
They also cover the app through `^production` when the e2e project depends on the app.

### Server setups

`@nx-ignore-server-inputs` works only when Nx starts your app server as an Nx task.
If Playwright or Cypress starts the server directly, this directive has no effect.
`@nx-depends-on` still limits the test's inputs to the projects you list, but excludes the server's inputs only when Nx starts it.

For **Playwright**, configure `webServer` as follows:

- Set `reuseExistingServer: true`, including in CI. Using `!process.env.CI` turns it off in CI.
- Set `command` to `nx run <project>:<target>` or `nx <target> <project>`, with no extra arguments. A prefix such as `npx` or `pnpm exec` is fine.
- Leave out `wait` and `env`.

For more on the commands Nx recognizes, see [Playwright web server readiness](/docs/kb/playwright-web-server-readiness).

For **Cypress**, set `ciWebServerCommand` in `nxE2EPreset` to an Nx command.
Don't set `webServerConfig.reuseExistingServer` to `false`.

**Jest** supports only `@nx-depends-on`. Neither directive is supported in **Vitest** or **Cypress component tests**.

Excluding the server's inputs also excludes its environment variables, runtime inputs, and external dependencies.
If the test needs any of these, add them to the test target's `inputs` in `project.json`.
Include `"..."` to keep the inputs Nx already provides.

### What `@nx-depends-on` infers

For `// @nx-depends-on: checkout, checkout-utils` in a Cypress test file, the plugin infers this configuration:

```jsonc
// apps/shop-e2e/project.json
{
  "targets": {
    "e2e-ci--src/e2e/checkout.cy.ts": {
      "inputs": [
        "default",
        {
          "input": "production",
          "projects": ["checkout", "checkout-utils"],
          "always": true,
        },
        {
          "input": "production",
          "projects": ["checkout", "checkout-utils"],
          "dependencies": true,
          "always": true,
        },
        { "externalDependencies": ["cypress"] },
      ],
      "dependsOn": [
        {
          "target": "serve-static",
          "projects": ["shop"],
          "waitFor": "ready",
          "inputs": false,
        },
      ],
    },
  },
}
```

- The two `production` inputs replace the `^` inputs the plugin infers: `^production` for Cypress, plus `^{projectRoot}/tsconfig*.json` for Playwright.
  They use `default` when there's no `production` [named input](/docs/kb/configure-inputs).
- `"always": true` keeps these projects in the hash under Ultracache, which would otherwise drop them because the test runner doesn't read the code the server serves.
- `"inputs": false` on the server dependency leaves the server task's inputs out of the hash.

### Scope in `project.json` or `nx.json`

You can write the same configuration in `project.json`, under the split target's name.
The `inputs` and `dependsOn` you set there replace the arrays the plugin infers, so copy every inferred entry you keep, and don't add the [spread token](/docs/reference/nx-json#spread-token) `"..."`, which restores `^production` and the server task's inputs.
The entry stops matching when you move or rename the test file, and each new test file needs its own entry.

A target default in `nx.json` that matches a split target, such as one keyed with a glob that starts with `e2e-ci--`, also replaces the inferred `inputs` and `dependsOn`.
To keep what a directive inferred, add `"..."` to each array the target default sets, and set `"inputs": false` yourself on any server dependency it adds.

### Lint and type-check the imports

The [`@nx/enforce-module-boundaries` rule](/docs/kb/enforce-module-boundaries#e2e-tests-that-hash-from-their-imports) skips most of its checks on bare imports of other projects in a test file with `@nx-ignore-server-inputs`.
It still checks imports that name values or types, and it runs every check on test files without that directive.

The `import/no-unassigned-import` rule of `eslint-plugin-import` and of the Oxlint import plugin reports bare imports.
Disable it for the e2e project's test files instead of removing the imports.

When a test file imports `.tsx` files, set `jsx` in the `compilerOptions` of the e2e project's TypeScript configuration, such as `"jsx": "react-jsx"`.
Without it, TypeScript reports error TS6305 (`Output file '<path>' has not been built from source file '<path>'`) with project references, or TS6142 with path aliases.
